export const CAPITAL_SYMMETRY: FleetText[] = [
  { domain: "holders-b1", text: "The same primary-filing lens applied to this batch's three fully-opened companies (Adani Enterprises, Adani Ports, Axis Bank) again produces a graded rather than uniform picture across the comparison set: BlackRock crosses the 1% named-holder threshold zero times in three checks (as in the base holders.json file's five checks); Vanguard, NBIM and GIC/Singapore each cross it exactly once, all three in the same company (Axis Bank) and all three below Dodge and Cox's 2.23% line in that same filing; Capital Group, Fidelity, KIA and ADIA cross it zero times. LIC, the domestic control, crosses 1% in all three companies checked — and in one of them (Axis Bank) LIC is not merely a large holder but the bank's sole promoter, a structurally different relationship no foreign holder in this batch has to any company. GQG Partners, outside the comparison set, is the largest non-domestic, non-promoter holder found in Adani Enterprises (3.87% combined) and the largest named FPI line in Adani Ports (1.93%) — both Adani group companies, both post-2023, consistent with the same concentrated pattern already observed in ITC in the base file. The largest single foreign lines in this batch by far are not passive FPI at all but promoter-group and strategic-FDI vehicles: the Adani family's own offshore 'Trade and Investment' cluster (15.68% of Adani Enterprises, 26.04% of Adani Ports) and IHC's Adani Enterprises stake (3.11%, once 5.04%) — both institutionally distinct from BlackRock/Vanguard-style passive index ownership and from ADIA-style sovereign portfolio investment, and both larger in Adani Ports than the entire public FPI Category I+II total (15.58%) combined." },
  { domain: "holders-b2", text: "The same lens (open the primary filing, list every named ≥1% foreign holder, name LIC as domestic control) was applied to all five batch-2 companies but could only be executed on one, Cipla, for source-access reasons documented in voids above — not because the other four were skipped. On Cipla, the result matches batch 1's pattern exactly: BlackRock is absent by name (as in all 5 of batch 1's checked companies), Vanguard and NBIM/Government Pension Fund Global each cross 1% via a single named fund line (1.01% and 1.98% respectively — both smaller than NBIM's 1.98% here was batch 1's Bharti Airtel line but larger than nothing in Reliance/ITC/SBI), GIC/Capital Group/Fidelity/KIA/ADIA are absent, and LIC's presence is not a clean 'LIC owns X%' figure but a LIC-*managed fund product* line (LICI ASM NON PAR, 6.15%) — the same pattern batch 1 found for LIC in Bharti Airtel (a ULIP fund, not LIC's own book) rather than the clean direct-holding figures batch 1 found for LIC in HDFC Bank, Reliance and ITC. The one asymmetry worth flagging: Cipla's Table II shows a foreign-promoter stake (20.05%, two NRI Hamied-family individuals) that dwarfs every institutional FPI line combined (24.55%) is close but the promoter figure alone nearly matches it — structurally different from batch 1's BAT/ITC and Suzuki/Maruti foreign-promoter findings in that here the foreign promoter is named individuals, not a corporate/FDI vehicle, which is exactly why CONTRACT rule 1 keeps it out of the claims table even though it is, by percentage, the most consequential foreign ownership fact in this file." },
  { domain: "holders-b3", text: "The same lens applied to this batch's five companies found LIC (the domestic control) named ≥1% and quantified in 2 of 3 companies actually opened at primary-source granularity — Eicher Motors (1.14%) and Grasim Industries (6.59%, the largest single named institutional shareholder in that company after the Birla promoter group) — and unnamed/below-threshold in the third (Eternal). This mirrors the companion holders.json file's finding: LIC clears 1% more often and by a wider margin than any single mandatory-comparison-set foreign holder. Of the mandatory comparison set, only two names surfaced anywhere in this batch: Capital Group (1.35% of Eicher Motors, via New World Fund Inc) and NBIM/Norges Bank (1.12% of Eternal, via Government Pension Fund Global) — both single sightings, both in the 1.0–1.4% range, neither approaching a control stake. BlackRock, Vanguard, Fidelity, Kuwait Investment Authority and ADIA were absent by name from every company where a primary filing was opened in this batch, consistent with the companion file's finding that passive-index and diversified-mandate holders rarely cross the 1% per-vehicle naming threshold in any single Indian company even when the underlying manager's aggregate India AUM is large. The batch's most concentrated non-domestic holder by far was not in the comparison set at all: Temasek Holdings, at a combined 2.87% of Eternal across two vehicles — larger than any comparison-set holder found in any of the eight companies opened across this file and its companion (holders.json) to date. Run on the two foreign promoter-linked holders found here — Suzuki Motor Corporation in the companion file's Maruti Suzuki sample, and P.T. Indo Bharat Rayon (part of the Birla family's own overseas promoter-group structure) in this batch's Grasim sample — the lens shows the same pattern twice: a single foreign-registered promoter/promoter-group vehicle dwarfs every passive FPI line in its own company, and in neither case is that vehicle a passive index fund of the kind the 'BlackRock owns India' narrative is about." },
  { domain: "holders-b4", text: "Run on the same three primary filings (HCL Technologies, HDFC Life, Hindalco), LIC appears as a named ≥1% domestic holder in exactly one of three (Hindalco, 4.59%) — in HCL Technologies and HDFC Life, the domestic 'Insurance Companies' line is disclosed only as an unbroken aggregate (8.32% across 39 holders; 2.20% across 30 holders respectively), with no individual name broken out, so LIC's absence there is a filing-format artefact, not evidence LIC holds nothing. The non-BlackRock comparison holders fared no better than BlackRock/Vanguard at crossing the 1% naming threshold: NBIM crossed it once (HDFC Life, 1.14%), GIC crossed it once (Hindalco, 2.65%), and Capital Group, Fidelity, Kuwait Investment Authority, ADIA and GQG Partners crossed it zero times across all three filings — the same fragmentation-across-many-registered-vehicles pattern documented in the main holders.json file recurs here. The two largest 'foreign' lines found in this batch by far are not portfolio investors at all: HCL Holdings Private Ltd (16.46% of HCL Technologies) is the founder family's own trust-held vehicle, and Morgan Guaranty Trust Company of New York (4.18% of Hindalco) is a GDR custodian bank holding shares on behalf of unnamed GDR holders. Read naively, a filing's raw 'Institutions (Foreign)' or Table II 'Foreign' aggregate therefore overstates genuine third-party foreign portfolio ownership by conflating family trusts and custodian balancing figures with actual outside investors — the same distortion the BAT/Suzuki promoter stakes illustrated in the main holders.json file, now shown to recur even inside the 'Foreign' promoter and depositary lines, not just the FPI lines." },
  { domain: "holders", text: "The same five-company, primary-filing lens applied to every name in SPEC's mandatory comparison set (BlackRock, Vanguard, NBIM, GIC/Government of Singapore, Capital Group, Fidelity, Kuwait Investment Authority, ADIA) plus LIC as the domestic control produces a graded, not uniform, picture: BlackRock — the name that carries the most alarming popular narrative — crosses the 1% named-holder threshold zero times in five checks; Vanguard once; NBIM and GIC/Singapore twice each; Capital Group, Fidelity, KIA and ADIA zero times each (absence-in-small-sample, not confirmed absence). LIC, the domestic control, crosses 1% in four of five and is the single largest non-promoter shareholder in three of them. The two foreign holdings that actually dwarf every passive-fund line in the sample — British American Tobacco's 22.91% of ITC and (tier reported) Suzuki Motor Corporation's ~58% of Maruti Suzuki — are neither BlackRock nor Vanguard nor any other passive index manager: they are foreign industrial promoters, a structurally different kind of foreign ownership the 'asset managers own India' narrative does not usually mean and does not distinguish. Running the same lens on the controls therefore does not produce an equally alarming picture for BlackRock specifically — if anything the opposite, a systematic UNDER-representation relative to its reputation, for the structural reason recorded on the cap:blackrock entity (fragmentation across many separately registered FPI vehicles, none individually crossing 1%) — while it produces a genuinely large, well-documented, non-alarmist finding (a legacy multinational tobacco company's ~23% stake in India's largest FMCG conglomerate; a Japanese automaker's ~59% stake in India's largest carmaker) that the popular narrative rarely names at all." },
  { domain: "mandates-ventures", text: "Advisers: the same mandate fields (client, deal, role, date, fee) were filled for Rothschild & Co and for the controls. Result: Rothschild has two dated mid-market mandates (Cropnosys Rs 375 crore, CleanMax US$360m) and a reported #3-by-count 2007–2023 rank; Morgan Stanley (Jio–Facebook US$5.7bn), Citi (#1 M&A and ECM H1 2024), Kotak (#1 fees H1 2024), Perella Weinberg (Holcim CHF 6.4bn) each have larger documented marquee mandates; Lazard and JP Morgan have none in this file and are equally absent from the H1 2024 tables. The lens that makes Rothschild look 'everywhere' makes it look smaller than eight named rivals once fees and values are used; the lens that makes it look 'nowhere' does the same to Lazard. Fees: undisclosed for every adviser on every side except the two Re 1 bids, which are Big Four, not Rothschild. JVs: Jio BlackRock, Bajaj Finserv, IIHL–Invesco and Nippon Life–RNAM were recorded with the same predicates (own, award, law) under the same reg. 7; the domestic control (Bajaj Finserv) waited the same 11 months for in-principle approval; the foreign-majority acquisitions (Nippon Life 75%, Invesco→IIHL 60%) went through change-in-control rather than a new licence, which is faster than either JV route. Persons: Ambani, Fink and Pandey were each recorded only by office with dates; none carries an allegation claim in this file." },
  { domain: "narratives-literature", text: "Three lenses were run on the controls named in the brief and on the mirror side. (1) 'Foreign holder owns X' — applied to Vanguard, NBIM, GIC, Capital Group, Fidelity and LIC on the same five primary filings (holders.json): the sovereign funds of Norway and Singapore cross 1% twice as often as BlackRock (which never does), and the domestic state insurer LIC crosses 1% in four of five — the lens, applied evenly, produces 'Norway and Singapore own India' or 'the Indian state owns India' before it produces 'BlackRock owns India'; the narrative selects its actor. (2) 'Foreign adviser controls X' — applied to Lazard, Goldman Sachs, Morgan Stanley, Citi, Kotak, Axis, Avendus (mandates-ventures.json): Rothschild & Co is absent from every LSEG H1 2024 top-10 where Citi, Kotak and Goldman appear, so the same lens run on the controls produces a more 'alarming' picture for Citi than for Rothschild; the narrative again selects its actor. (3) 'Coordinated foreign-funded campaign' — the identical test (common funder + same-week statements + shared target + political reading) applied to the response to Hindenburg's Aug 2024 report shows AMFI (the regulated industry), the Adani Group, a BJP office-holder, a senior lawyer, a former Infosys CFO and India's then IMF executive director issuing mutually reinforcing statements within 48 hours, carried by the state broadcaster (newsonair.gov.in) — which under the same lens is 'coordination' by state-funded media of a state-aligned message. Neither side's simultaneity is evidence of direction; both are the base rate of public controversy. The lens produces an equally alarming picture for the controls in all three cases; that is a finding about the lens, and the narratives are calibrated accordingly (unsupported/debunked for the actor-selecting claims; contested only where a documented structure exists without a numerator — Mauritius round-tripping — or where the record is genuinely two-sided — OCCRP's US funding, the 'clean chit')." },
  { domain: "rules-regulators", text: "The brief's control is 'the same disclosure lens on domestic promoters/other FPI-holding groups.' SEBI's own circulars and board minutes state the 50%-concentration and size criteria in group-neutral terms and were, on SEBI's account, triggered by roughly Rs 2.6 lakh crore of FPI AUM as of March 2023 (base rate above) — far more than Adani group companies alone could account for, implying other groups were caught. The March 2024 no-identified-promoter exemption is the clearest documented evidence the rule is built around promoter-linked concentration generically: it is drafted to exclude exactly the companies (those without an identifiable promoter) that structurally cannot present the round-tripping risk the whole regime targets, and by that same logic it cannot apply to Adani group companies, which do have identified promoters. Where the lens fails the symmetry test is transparency, not design: SEBI has never named which non-Adani groups or FPIs were caught, so the claim that the rule bites broadly cannot be verified against named cases from public sources — only inferred from the aggregate AUM figure and the rule's own neutral drafting. That gap cuts both ways: it is equally consistent with a genuinely broad, Adani-including-but-not-Adani-only regime, and with a regime that in practice concentrates its bite on the one group whose FPIs drew press and judicial attention. This file cannot resolve that from what SEBI has published." },
];

