        "denialFound": "None found. Both parties were asked: 'Both Deloitte and SBI Caps did not respond to emailed queries' (BS, 4 Dec 2019, https://www.business-standard.com/article/companies/bpcl-sake-sale-deloitte-quotes-just-re-1-as-its-disinvestment-advisory-fee-119120400063_1.html). I found no later correction or contrary report. A new web search was not possible because the search budget was exhausted, so contrary reporting was not searched beyond the repo and the article itself.",
        "innocentReading": "A loss-leader bid for prestige: a near-zero fee buys a place on India's largest strategic sale. BS carries this explanation twice. An unnamed source says 'For Deloitte, this may be a prestigious deal and will add to its portfolio a national energy company'. Partha Bhattacharya, Coal India chairman at the time of its IPO, says bankers 'don't look at the cash money... could have gone for a notional token figure to get their name associated with this'. Under L1 (lowest-bidder) selection, a Re 1 bid wins on price.",
        "reason": "The source was fetched with curl after WebFetch returned 403, and it says what the claim says. Quotes: 'Deloitte Touche Tohmatsu India has quoted Rs 1 to manage the sale of Bharat Petroleum Corporation'; 'According to three persons in the know, SBI Caps was the second-lowest bidder with a quote in the range of Rs 15-17 crore. Both Deloitte and SBI Caps did not respond to emailed queries'; 'On November 28, both Deloitte and SBI Caps had made presentations before the Department of Investment and Public Asset Management, after which the former was selected the successful bidder.' The award itself is documented separately by DIPAM's EoI (c003). The fee rests on one credible outlet with a named reporter and anonymous sources, and has no primary confirmation, so 'reported' is the ceiling. The content survives both lenses. The edge encoding does not, and needs the fixes listed in corrections.",
