        "denialFound": "OCCRP's response appears in the Drop Site piece: restrictions like these \"are common across all government grants\" and \"no donor has ever vetoed any staff position.\" Drew Sullivan disputed the method and put the figure at 46.4% after excluding pass-through grants. The OCCRP FAQ (https://www.occrp.org/en/frequently-asked-questions-on-occrps-funding-and-editorial-policies, updated 7 Dec 2024) says \"Donors never have access to editorial meetings or any aspect of our editorial process, nor do they have input into what stories we publish.\" The same FAQ admits the substance of the key-personnel point: \"the donor needs to agree that the replacement is qualified.\" The State Department denied influencing editorial decisions (ThePrint, 7 Dec 2024). OCCRP was asked before publication, and its denial is recorded as c017.",
        "innocentReading": "Western governments are the main funders of cross-border investigative non-profits in regions without domestic philanthropy. Standard US federal cooperative agreements require the grantor to approve changes to named key personnel (2 CFR 200.308). Neither fact shows that a government commissioned or steered any story.",
